finding: Verification defect: the fragment-stage color oracle shares the production decode, so an incorrect blue channel can pass every stream check. src/standard/rendering/mesh-streams.gpu.test.ts:190-192 computes decodedColor with production decodeColor, then uses decodedColor.z as the expected fragment blue (lines 225-226). examples/gltf/tests/parity.ts:196-203 likewise decodes both actual and expected with decodeColor. The only independent decodeColor checks, src/core/mesh/streams.test.ts:49-51, assert x and w, with identical first/second packed words, and never assert z. Thus changing decodeColor (src/core/mesh/vertex.ts:87) to return RGAA instead of RGBA would corrupt every colored mesh's blue in the fragment stage while these checks still pass. This is the same shared-reading failure mode the review was allocated to challenge. Add independent four-channel expectations using distinct packed words and direct source-color quantization bounds (not production decodeColor) in the GPU check. No implementation misdecode found by inspection; this finding is against the stage's promised test sensitivity. No files changed or commits made.
